 number the officer has to question, and questions cost weeks."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"5fafd3be-caa5-471b-a528-f93377f021cc","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The five layers, in the order they stack","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"f9acefc0-073c-4268-bc2c-c010613aee5f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"The words below get used loosely in conversation and precisely in an audit. This is the precise version."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"9e6275d7-11db-4d82-98a8-8cf48366a808","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Direct labor — hours × the actual hourly rate of the people doing the work. Not a blended \"engineer rate\" you invented; the rate you really pay."},{"text":"Fringe benefits — the employer's share of payroll taxes, health insurance, paid leave, retirement. Applied to direct labor."},{"text":"Overhead — the cost of having an engineering operation at all: facilities, equipment depreciation, engineering management. Applied to labor plus fringe."},{"text":"G&A (general and administrative) — running the company: accounting, legal, executive time, business development. Applied to nearly everything above it, and usually to materials and subcontracts too."},{"text":"Fee — profit. On a Phase I, agencies typically expect something in the range of 7%, applied last."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"db37c10a-bafd-45a7-91a3-1393d9b10698","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"The order matters because each layer applies to a base that includes the layers under it. Change one direct labor hour and every number below it moves. That is why a cost volume assembled by hand in a spreadsheet the night before is fragile: one late change to the staffing plan silently breaks four subtotals.","inline_formats":[{"start":50,"format":"italic","length":4}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"f8d8c701-4e8b-4d61-8ba2-d88943e4f588","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Where the money actually goes","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"d28d7a6b-9ed1-4a66-88ac-1c4b7f053c28","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"A useful sanity check before you submit: a Phase I where direct labor is a small fraction of the total is a proposal that will draw questions. The government is buying research effort. If most of your budget is materials, subcontracts, or travel, be ready to explain why — and put that explanation in the budget narrative rather than making the officer ask."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"8aa62860-8965-4b2f-883b-685401cec330","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The rules that are not negotiable","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"b967cd62-256f-4cef-966d-94416e4c7b4c","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Subcontract limits. SBIR Phase I generally requires that at least two thirds of the work be performed by the small business. STTR splits differently: at least 40% by the small business and at least 30% by the research institution. Blow through the limit and you are ineligible, however good the science is."},{"text":"The ceiling is a ceiling. If the solicitation says a Phase I may not exceed a stated amount, that is the total including fee — not the amount before fee."},{"text":"Cost sharing. SBIR does not require it, and volunteering it rarely helps. Read the solicitation before you offer anything."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"39d3d9de-c54b-45b5-b865-eab4b6562fa1","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The five mistakes that cost awards","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"0e060ccd-a704-4236-bcb5-16320f33452b","type":"numbered_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"The total on the summary does not match the detail. Almost always a stale number left behind by an edit. This is the single most common reason a cost volume comes back."},{"text":"A rate applied to the wrong base. G&A on top of fee, or overhead applied to materials when your disclosed rate structure does not do that."},{"text":"Labor categories that do not exist in the technical volume. If the cost volume budgets a \"Senior Optics Engineer\" for 400 hours, the technical volume's staffing section had better name one."},{"text":"Unsupported \"other direct costs\". A line called Miscellaneous is an invitation to a question."},{"text":"A work split that violates the subcontract limit — usually by accident, when a university partner's scope grows during writing and nobody re-checks the percentage."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"f2a2d05b-8bc8-41d0-a9e6-30c1cd81e0f6","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"How this platform helps","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"0e9ce81c-29ff-4a30-861c-7f2f3e23f5cd","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"The cost volume here is computed, not typed. You enter the staffing plan and the rates once; the burden engine applies each layer to its correct base and produces the totals, so the arithmetic cannot drift when you change an assumption. The result is then rendered into the form the solicitation actually requires — a DoD/DoW burden waterfall, an NSF/DOE SF-424A, or a state EDA budget form — rather than a generic table you would have to transcribe.","inline_formats":[{"start":24,"format":"bold","length":19},{"start":274,"format":"bold","length":39}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"0f8d2a98-e774-4cc9-af5d-7b475fed8df2","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"The work split is calculated from the same plan, so the two-thirds rule is checked as you build rather than discovered at submission. And because the numbers flow from one model, editing a cell updates the roll-up and the export together."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"cdd20555-748a-4bec-87ae-aa2fdf235906","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Two things it does not do for you. It cannot invent your indirect rates — those come from your accounting system, and if you have a negotiated rate agreement you must use it. And it cannot tell you whether a cost is allowable under your particular award; that is a conversation with your accountant, and it is worth having before the first Phase I, not after the first audit.","inline_formats":[{"start":0,"format":"bold","length":34}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false}]}],"layout":{"mode":"flow"}}],"document_id":"8d830e6c-1bdb-411f-8036-1ebfa1d339a5"},"excerpt":"Direct labor, fringe, overhead, G&A, fee — what each rate is, how the numbers have to tie out, and the mistakes that cost a technically strong proposal its award.","externalUrl":null,"featuredImage":null}$g168b$::jsonb, $g168b$Drafted follow-on guide — queued for review (#168)$g168b$, $g168b$eric@rfppipeline.com$g168b$, now())
