INSERT INTO content_pages (id, page_key, content_type, version_no, status, title, blocks, metadata, audit_note, created_by, created_at)
VALUES ('51e7ecb4-6557-4580-8706-01d745767a02', $g168b$sbir-phase-two-transition$g168b$, 'guide', 1, 'draft', $g168b$Planning Phase II While You Write Phase I$g168b$, $g168b$[{"body":"<h1 style=\"font-size:1.875rem;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin:1.5rem 0 .75rem\">Planning Phase II While You Write Phase I</h1>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">A Phase I award is roughly six months and a modest amount of money. A Phase II is typically two years and an order of magnitude more. Almost every company that wins one describes the same thing afterwards: <strong>Phase II was decided during Phase I</strong>, and mostly by choices that seemed minor at the time.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">This is what those choices are.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">What Phase II is actually judged on</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Phase I asks whether the idea is feasible. Phase II asks a harder pair of questions: <em>did you show it</em>, and <em>will anyone use it</em>. The second one is where technically excellent teams lose.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Reviewers are looking for three things you can only have if you planned for them:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;margin:0 0 1rem\"><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">Evidence, not assertion. A Phase I final report that says the approach &quot;showed promise&quot; is weaker than one with a measured number against a stated baseline — even a modest number.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">A named transition path. Who takes this next? A program office, a prime contractor, a commercial buyer. &quot;The DoD&quot; is not an answer; a program is.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">Something outside your own building. A letter of support, a CRADA, a pilot, a purchase intent. Interest that costs someone else something is worth more than interest that does not.</li></ul>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">The four decisions to make in month one</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\"><strong>1. Define the metric you will report before you start measuring.</strong> Write down, in the Phase I proposal, the specific number that will constitute success and the baseline you are comparing it against. Teams that do this have a clean result to report. Teams that do not end up describing what they built rather than what they proved.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\"><strong>2. Find the transition customer while the work is easy to describe.</strong> The best time to talk to a program office is when you have a funded effort and no results yet — you are interesting and you are not asking for anything. Six months later you are asking them to sponsor a Phase II on a deadline. Start those conversations in month one.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\"><strong>3. Line up the letters early.</strong> A letter of support takes an organization weeks to produce and ten minutes to promise. Ask in month two for a letter you need in month six. And ask for specificity: a letter that names the capability and says what the organization would do with it carries weight; a form letter does not.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\"><strong>4. Keep the commercialization story growing from day one.</strong> Phase II asks for a commercialization plan, and it is scored. Written at the end, it reads like a projection. Built through the effort — market conversations logged, pricing tested, a competitor mapped, a partner engaged — it reads like a plan, because it is one.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">Direct to Phase II</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Some agencies allow a <strong>Direct to Phase II (D2P2)</strong> submission: you skip Phase I entirely by demonstrating that you have already done equivalent feasibility work with your own or other funding. It is a genuine route, and it is more demanding than it sounds — you must document the prior work in enough detail that a reviewer can conclude the Phase I question is already answered.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">D2P2 is worth considering if you have relevant internal R&amp;D, a prior contract, or academic work you can point to. It is not a shortcut for a project that has not been de-risked; it is a way to get credit for de-risking you already did.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">The gap between the phases</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Phase I ends and Phase II funding does not begin immediately. The interval is usually months, and it is where small companies lose the team they just assembled.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Two things help. Some agencies and states run <strong>bridge or matching programs</strong> specifically to cover it — worth researching <em>before</em> Phase I ends rather than during the gap. And the transition customer you found in month one is the person most likely to help accelerate the process, because they want the capability.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">How this platform helps</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Your Phase I proposal, its final report, and the material you generated along the way stay in your library and remain reusable — so the Phase II technical volume starts from what you actually wrote and proved, not from a blank page. Past-performance narratives, staffing plans, letters of support and figures are all retrievable into the new build, with their provenance intact.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">The opportunity spine also tracks the Phase II window against the same opportunity, so the follow-on shows up on your board rather than in your memory.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">What it will not do is have the conversations for you. The transition customer, the letters, and the pilot are yours to go and get — and month one is when to start.</p>","title":"Planning Phase II While You Write Phase I","excerpt":"Phase II is won by the decisions you make in Phase I — the data you collect, the partners you line up, and the commercialization story you start building on day one.","section":"body","metadata":{}}]$g168b$::jsonb, $g168b${"tags":["Phase II","commercialization","SBIR","strategy"],"author":null,"canvas":{"nodes":[],"canvas":{"width":612,"footer":null,"format":"custom","header":null,"height":792,"margins":{"top":72,"left":72,"right":72,"bottom":72},"max_pages":null,"max_slides":null,"font_default":{"size":12,"family":"Times New Roman"},"line_spacing":1.15},"version":2,"metadata":{"title":"Planning Phase II While You Write Phase I","status":"in_progress","volume_id":"","created_at":"","proposal_id":"","version_number":1,"solicitation_id":"","last_modified_at":"","last_modified_by":"","required_item_id":""},"sections":[{"id":"58c67d80-d928-4036-902a-563895abc7a6","title":"Planning Phase II While You Write Phase I","groups":[{"id":"242ccb8e-85bd-4e29-b317-488e1c5563c3","nodes":[{"id":"b5eff424-923a-4028-b50a-4c2648efc6f3","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Planning Phase II While You Write Phase I","level":1},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"06571dc4-0137-4567-9f4c-8190f85e9485","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"A Phase I award is roughly six months and a modest amount of money. A Phase II is typically two years and an order of magnitude more. Almost every company that wins one describes the same thing afterwards: Phase II was decided during Phase I, and mostly by choices that seemed minor at the time.","inline_formats":[{"start":206,"format":"bold","length":35}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"b7470e06-0157-4a5f-86ee-3d6d17dfcfae","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"This is what those choices are."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"c0d0ef7b-f7af-4679-a72d-1d21b8d221bd","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"What Phase II is actually judged on","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"b88afee5-174e-4343-aa69-8ace5dc2041b","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Phase I asks whether the idea is feasible. Phase II asks a harder pair of questions: did you show it, and will anyone use it. The second one is where technically excellent teams lose.","inline_formats":[{"start":85,"format":"italic","length":15},{"start":106,"format":"italic","length":18}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"c2e7ac11-d9c9-48de-aa40-2426ff3c97bc","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Reviewers are looking for three things you can only have if you planned for them:"},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"b501acbb-b76b-4271-8fa3-c2ce06f00482","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"Evidence, not assertion. A Phase I final report that says the approach \"showed promise\" is weaker than one with a measured number against a stated baseline — even a modest number."},{"text":"A named transition path. Who takes this next? A program office, a prime contractor, a commercial buyer. \"The DoD\" is not an answer; a program is."},{"text":"Something outside your own building. A letter of support, a CRADA, a pilot, a purchase intent. Interest that costs someone else something is worth more than interest that does not."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"349d0225-5ac3-4a00-b080-216d47b4ce81","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The four decisions to make in month one","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"3889ebed-adc0-4b09-8d7d-f9a29c44e200","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"1. Define the metric you will report before you start measuring. Write down, in the Phase I proposal, the specific number that will constitute success and the baseline you are comparing it against. Teams that do this have a clean result to report. Teams that do not end up describing what they built rather than what they proved.","inline_formats":[{"start":0,"format":"bold","length":64}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"b0c9f7b8-dcec-4f93-ab8a-9fc611444208","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"2. Find the transition customer while the work is easy to describe. The best time to talk to a program office is when you have a funded effort and no results yet — you are interesting and you are not asking for anything. Six months later you are asking them to sponsor a Phase II on a deadline. Start those conversations in month one.","inline_formats":[{"start":0,"format":"bold","length":67}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"f01d6996-0084-4d03-8518-6e0fc1ec3343","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"3. Line up the letters early. A letter of support takes an organization weeks to produce and ten minutes to promise. Ask in month two for a letter you need in month six. And ask for specificity: a letter that names the capability and says what the organization would do with it carries weight; a form letter does not.","inline_formats":[{"start":0,"format":"bold","length":29}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"5f1bc5e7-0dcf-4914-b90d-27fb8792099f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"4. Keep the commercialization story growing from day one. Phase II asks for a commercialization plan, and it is scored. Written at the end, it reads like a projection. Built through the effort — market conversations logged, pricing tested, a competitor mapped, a partner engaged — it reads like a plan, because it is one.","inline_formats":[{"start":0,"format":"bold","length":57}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"8b5869f7-7b71-473e-9ec5-833c6ca41553","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Direct to Phase II","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"5ad95d56-6912-4408-a2c3-48c757c04b81","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Some agencies allow a Direct to Phase II (D2P2) submission: you skip Phase I entirely by demonstrating that you have already done equivalent feasibility work with your own or other funding. It is a genuine route, and it is more demanding than it sounds — you must document the prior work in enough detail that a reviewer can conclude the Phase I question is already answered.","inline_formats":[{"start":22,"format":"bold","length":25}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"de03a7c7-d775-4ff3-b2ec-00e591973861","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"D2P2 is worth considering if you have relevant internal R&D, a prior contract, or academic work you can point to. It is not a shortcut for a project that has not been de-risked; it is a way to get credit for de-risking you already did."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"dfa14348-874a-4c82-8dde-01426ee2165c","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The gap between the phases","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"19ae84ca-7875-4ede-8086-5c961975451e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Phase I ends and Phase II funding does not begin immediately. The interval is usually months, and it is where small companies lose the team they just assembled."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"c5e50084-e5a8-4a63-8fa0-c8013a7d1be8","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Two things help. Some agencies and states run bridge or matching programs specifically to cover it — worth researching before Phase I ends rather than during the gap. And the transition customer you found in month one is the person most likely to help accelerate the process, because they want the capability.","inline_formats":[{"start":46,"format":"bold","length":27},{"start":119,"format":"italic","length":6}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"cfe5ef13-676b-478d-9325-6297f2bed90f","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"How this platform helps","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"8f2cb255-2af0-41bb-96ab-61df1fd74937","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Your Phase I proposal, its final report, and the material you generated along the way stay in your library and remain reusable — so the Phase II technical volume starts from what you actually wrote and proved, not from a blank page. Past-performance narratives, staffing plans, letters of support and figures are all retrievable into the new build, with their provenance intact."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"1da2631d-4abf-424c-a51e-74563e974cdb","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"The opportunity spine also tracks the Phase II window against the same opportunity, so the follow-on shows up on your board rather than in your memory."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"574fccb4-ade5-4748-843d-3a7ac121f7a4","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"What it will not do is have the conversations for you. The transition customer, the letters, and the pilot are yours to go and get — and month one is when to start."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false}]}],"layout":{"mode":"flow"}}],"document_id":"beecb032-f48c-4220-a483-3ec51f85b07e"},"excerpt":"Phase II is won by the decisions you make in Phase I — the data you collect, the partners you line up, and the commercialization story you start building on day one.","externalUrl":null,"featuredImage":null}$g168b$::jsonb, $g168b$Drafted follow-on guide — queued for review (#168)$g168b$, $g168b$eric@rfppipeline.com$g168b$, now())
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO content_pages (id, page_key, content_type, version_no, status, title, blocks, metadata, audit_note, created_by, created_at)
VALUES ('2b5d115b-0665-4b84-a0c7-182840dcd914', $g168b$teaming-and-subcontracts$g168b$, 'guide', 1, 'draft', $g168b$Teaming: Subcontractors, Consultants, and the STTR Research Partner$g168b$, $g168b$[{"body":"<h1 style=\"font-size:1.875rem;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin:1.5rem 0 .75rem\">Teaming: Subcontractors, Consultants, and the Research Partner</h1>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">You rarely have every skill a project needs in-house, and you are not expected to. What SBIR and STTR <em>do</em> expect is that the small business remains the one doing the work — and both programs enforce that with hard numbers. Get the split wrong and you are non-compliant, no matter how good the team is.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">The work-split rules</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">For <strong>SBIR</strong>, the small business concern must perform a minimum share of the research and analytical effort itself: at least <strong>two-thirds in Phase I</strong>, and at least <strong>one-half in Phase II</strong>. The rest may go to subcontractors, consultants and other partners.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">For <strong>STTR</strong>, the structure is different by design — the program exists to move research out of institutions and into companies. The small business must perform at least <strong>40 percent</strong> of the work, and the single partnering <strong>research institution</strong> at least <strong>30 percent</strong>. The remaining 30 percent may be done by either partner or by additional third parties.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Two things about these percentages. They are measured on the <strong>research and analytical effort</strong>, which in practice usually means cost or labour as the solicitation defines it — read that definition rather than assuming. And an agency may impose a stricter split than the floor, so the solicitation always wins over the general rule.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">Who your principal investigator works for</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">For <strong>SBIR</strong>, the PI's <strong>primary employment</strong> must be with the small business — more than half their time during the project. A professor who intends to stay primarily at the university cannot be your SBIR PI.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">For <strong>STTR</strong>, the PI may be primarily employed by <em>either</em> the small business or the research partner. This is one of the most useful practical differences between the two programs, and it is the reason a university-anchored team is often an STTR team.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">The STTR research partner</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">STTR requires a <strong>single</strong> partnering research institution — a university, a federally funded R&amp;D center, or a qualifying nonprofit research organisation. Before you submit you will generally need:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;margin:0 0 1rem\"><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">A written agreement between the company and the institution covering the allocation of rights in intellectual property and rights to carry out follow-on research.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">The institution's own budget and rates, which are frequently very different from yours and can consume a share of the award you did not expect.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">Sign-off from the institution's sponsored-programs office, which is a real approval with a real queue. Start it weeks out, not days.</li></ul>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">Subcontractor versus consultant</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Both count against your outside share, but they document differently. A <strong>subcontractor</strong> is an organisation; expect to provide their statement of work, budget and basis of estimate, and sometimes a letter of commitment. A <strong>consultant</strong> is an individual, usually priced at a daily or hourly rate, and agencies commonly cap consultant rates or require justification above a threshold.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">For either, get it in writing <strong>before</strong> you submit: scope, price, period of performance, and what happens to IP they touch. A commitment letter that says &quot;we look forward to collaborating&quot; is not a subcontract, and a partner who becomes unavailable after award is your problem, not the agency's.</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">What to have in hand before the deadline</h2>\n<ul style=\"list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;margin:0 0 1rem\"><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">A statement of work for every subcontractor, specific enough to price.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">Their budget, in enough detail that your cost volume can carry it as a line.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">Letters of commitment or support where the solicitation asks for them — noting whether they count against your page limit.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">For STTR, the executed or agreed allocation-of-rights agreement.</li><li style=\"margin:.25rem 0\">A calculation, written down, showing your work split satisfies the program minimum.</li></ul>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem\">How this platform helps</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Subcontractors and consultants are line items the cost engine carries through the burden build-up, so the work split is computed from the same numbers as the price rather than estimated separately — and it rolls into submission readiness alongside your page limits and required documents.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;line-height:1.7\">Partners can also work in the build itself. A collaborator is invited with <strong>stage-scoped access</strong> — view, comment, or edit, on the specific parts of the proposal you grant and nothing else — so a university partner can draft their section without seeing the rest of your bid. What they upload lands as a draft for your review before it enters anything.</p>","title":"Teaming: Subcontractors, Consultants, and the STTR Research Partner","excerpt":"How much of the work you are allowed to send outside your company, what a research partner changes, and what to get in writing before you submit.","section":"body","metadata":{}}]$g168b$::jsonb, $g168b${"tags":["teaming","subcontracts","STTR","compliance"],"author":null,"canvas":{"nodes":[],"canvas":{"width":612,"footer":null,"format":"custom","header":null,"height":792,"margins":{"top":72,"left":72,"right":72,"bottom":72},"max_pages":null,"max_slides":null,"font_default":{"size":12,"family":"Times New Roman"},"line_spacing":1.15},"version":2,"metadata":{"title":"Teaming: Subcontractors, Consultants, and the STTR Research Partner","status":"in_progress","volume_id":"","created_at":"","proposal_id":"","version_number":1,"solicitation_id":"","last_modified_at":"","last_modified_by":"","required_item_id":""},"sections":[{"id":"2010b02c-4b62-44ab-a623-dadde91b7b5a","title":"Teaming: Subcontractors, Consultants, and the STTR Research Partner","groups":[{"id":"3206bd89-66a1-47dd-91a5-0e93d93c5112","nodes":[{"id":"3a3d6a3b-ab3a-44e7-87b0-ff9a0bd39a7e","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Teaming: Subcontractors, Consultants, and the Research Partner","level":1},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"1e0ab981-0df8-4700-a7da-ddc7c414d44f","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"You rarely have every skill a project needs in-house, and you are not expected to. What SBIR and STTR do expect is that the small business remains the one doing the work — and both programs enforce that with hard numbers. Get the split wrong and you are non-compliant, no matter how good the team is.","inline_formats":[{"start":102,"format":"italic","length":2}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"7e74d41c-c6c1-46f1-9504-756b399090ab","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The work-split rules","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"0df42cc2-37fd-4c14-a2d5-6e7e8392a4a1","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"For SBIR, the small business concern must perform a minimum share of the research and analytical effort itself: at least two-thirds in Phase I, and at least one-half in Phase II. The rest may go to subcontractors, consultants and other partners.","inline_formats":[{"start":4,"format":"bold","length":4},{"start":121,"format":"bold","length":21},{"start":157,"format":"bold","length":20}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"ee15f6e0-8d8c-4312-a63b-0c4b97cef331","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"For STTR, the structure is different by design — the program exists to move research out of institutions and into companies. The small business must perform at least 40 percent of the work, and the single partnering research institution at least 30 percent. The remaining 30 percent may be done by either partner or by additional third parties.","inline_formats":[{"start":4,"format":"bold","length":4},{"start":166,"format":"bold","length":10},{"start":216,"format":"bold","length":20},{"start":246,"format":"bold","length":10}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"2a5b9e0a-5862-4238-b0f3-f892227a6521","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Two things about these percentages. They are measured on the research and analytical effort, which in practice usually means cost or labour as the solicitation defines it — read that definition rather than assuming. And an agency may impose a stricter split than the floor, so the solicitation always wins over the general rule.","inline_formats":[{"start":61,"format":"bold","length":30}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"ae77190a-70fb-40d2-a573-1b8679d53770","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Who your principal investigator works for","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"cd4f3fef-183f-46ad-8e54-35edf5958d67","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"For SBIR, the PI's primary employment must be with the small business — more than half their time during the project. A professor who intends to stay primarily at the university cannot be your SBIR PI.","inline_formats":[{"start":4,"format":"bold","length":4},{"start":19,"format":"bold","length":18}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"fbaf6b5b-c540-4209-8e68-fbc74c6e7502","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"For STTR, the PI may be primarily employed by either the small business or the research partner. This is one of the most useful practical differences between the two programs, and it is the reason a university-anchored team is often an STTR team.","inline_formats":[{"start":4,"format":"bold","length":4},{"start":46,"format":"italic","length":6}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"09f51b59-24ee-4a05-a663-32d80caec9d2","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"The STTR research partner","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"2dbb8028-46f4-4052-b3f8-81b1a5798562","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"STTR requires a single partnering research institution — a university, a federally funded R&D center, or a qualifying nonprofit research organisation. Before you submit you will generally need:","inline_formats":[{"start":16,"format":"bold","length":6}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"d5bfeaec-cbc4-4c6d-8a3e-8c34dd750bbe","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"A written agreement between the company and the institution covering the allocation of rights in intellectual property and rights to carry out follow-on research."},{"text":"The institution's own budget and rates, which are frequently very different from yours and can consume a share of the award you did not expect."},{"text":"Sign-off from the institution's sponsored-programs office, which is a real approval with a real queue. Start it weeks out, not days."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"6aec0eb5-63b9-40ed-a0bc-f8a518966ea2","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Subcontractor versus consultant","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"d5148f9f-9c5d-49e0-a154-3b915f80cd41","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Both count against your outside share, but they document differently. A subcontractor is an organisation; expect to provide their statement of work, budget and basis of estimate, and sometimes a letter of commitment. A consultant is an individual, usually priced at a daily or hourly rate, and agencies commonly cap consultant rates or require justification above a threshold.","inline_formats":[{"start":72,"format":"bold","length":13},{"start":219,"format":"bold","length":10}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"c4c141a7-adfc-4faf-aa10-5d451c9ed4b7","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"For either, get it in writing before you submit: scope, price, period of performance, and what happens to IP they touch. A commitment letter that says \"we look forward to collaborating\" is not a subcontract, and a partner who becomes unavailable after award is your problem, not the agency's.","inline_formats":[{"start":30,"format":"bold","length":6}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"e75f0ba6-dbc4-4851-88c5-70b62cb3147e","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"What to have in hand before the deadline","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"8d817e2c-d5b1-4ece-a025-b815f85d2d6c","type":"bulleted_list","style":{},"content":{"items":[{"text":"A statement of work for every subcontractor, specific enough to price."},{"text":"Their budget, in enough detail that your cost volume can carry it as a line."},{"text":"Letters of commitment or support where the solicitation asks for them — noting whether they count against your page limit."},{"text":"For STTR, the executed or agreed allocation-of-rights agreement."},{"text":"A calculation, written down, showing your work split satisfies the program minimum."}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"29e9c174-7a21-4af6-a15a-880bde70b5a4","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"How this platform helps","level":2},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"8da20302-6ce6-4811-aa38-4c231b075aad","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Subcontractors and consultants are line items the cost engine carries through the burden build-up, so the work split is computed from the same numbers as the price rather than estimated separately — and it rolls into submission readiness alongside your page limits and required documents."},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false},{"id":"44ed03af-21db-4ac9-90b2-91c535aa494e","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"Partners can also work in the build itself. A collaborator is invited with stage-scoped access — view, comment, or edit, on the specific parts of the proposal you grant and nothing else — so a university partner can draft their section without seeing the rest of your bid. What they upload lands as a draft for your review before it enters anything.","inline_formats":[{"start":75,"format":"bold","length":19}]},"history":[],"provenance":{"source":"template"},"library_eligible":false}]}],"layout":{"mode":"flow"}}],"document_id":"16438894-dc47-4d0c-af1d-fd0e0ce43f05"},"excerpt":"How much of the work you are allowed to send outside your company, what a research partner changes, and what to get in writing before you submit.","externalUrl":null,"featuredImage":null}$g168b$::jsonb, $g168b$Drafted practice guide — queued for review (#168 wave 3)$g168b$, $g168b$eric@rfppipeline.com$g168b$, now())
